
--->Problem Statement <----
You are given an array prices where prices[i] is the price of a given stock on the i th day.
You want to maximize your profit by choosing a single day to buy one stock and choosing a different day in 
the future to sell that stock. Return the maximum profit you can achieve from this transaction. 
If you cannot achieve any profit, return 0.
--->APPROUCHES--> BRUTE FORCE <---
Using nested for loop :
To find the maximum profit, we inspect all possible (buy,sell) pairs using nested for loop.
1. Initialize profit = 0 and n = length of price array.
2. Run an outer loop i from 0 to n-1 to choose the buying day.
3. Run an inner loop j from i+1 to n-1 to choose the selling day.
4. Calculate profit = max(profit, prices[j] - prices[i]).
5. After checking all pairs, return the final profit.

---->CODE<---
public class Main {
   static class Solution {
       public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
           int profit = 0;
           int n = prices.length;
           // Try every pair of buy and sell days
           for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
               int buy = prices[i];
               for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                   int sell = prices[j];
                   // Update profit if current pair gives better profit
                   profit = Math.max(profit, sell - buy);
               }
           }
           return profit;
       }
   }
Time Complexity = O(n^2)
Space Complexity = O(1)

     --->APPROUCHES--> Optimized <---
Single pass approach -->
We track the lowest buying price and update the profit only when a better selling opportunity appears after it.
1. Initialize buy = prices[0] and profit = 0 to track the best buying price and max profit.
2. Since we already buy the stock at index 0 (the first day), we start traversing
the array from index 1 (the second day) to index n - 1 (the last day) to evaluate each subsequent stock price.
If prices[i] < buy, update buy with the lower price.
3. Else if prices[i] - buy > profit, update profit with the new max profit.
4. Continue this process to track the lowest buy and highest sell combination.
5. Return the final profit, which holds the maximum achievable profit.
Dry Run
Input: prices = [7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4]
Initialization: Buy = prices[0] , Profit = 0
Result:
Buy on Day 1 at Price = 1
Sell on Day 4 at Price = 6
Profit = 6 - 1 = 5

 ----> CODE <---
public class Main {
   static class Solution {
       public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
         // Initialize the buying price with the first day's price
       int buyPrice = prices[0];
       int maxProfit = 0;
       // Traverse the array from the second day to the last day
       for (int i = 1; i < prices.length; i++) {
           // If we find a lower price, update the buy value
           if (prices[i] < buyPrice) {
               buyPrice = prices[i];
           }
           // If selling today gives a better profit, update the profit
           else if (prices[i] - buyPrice > maxProfit) {
               maxProfit = prices[i] - buyPrice;
           }
       }
       // Return the maximum profit found
       return maxProfit;
       }
   }
Time Complexity = O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)

